    slug: "reject-a-still-that-changed-color",
    title: "Reject a Still That Changed Color",
    excerpt: "If a still changes the product color, the cap, or the flavor you sell, reject it. A nicer grade is a different product.",
    locale: "en",
    landingPath: "/product-promo",
    tags: product,
    scene: "A plain product bottle in its true simple color on a table, blank label, one shadow, no text",
    cta: "Compare the still to the real pack",
    answer: "Reject a still that changed the color. Put it next to the real pack or the real photo and check the hue, the cap, and the liquid if you can see it. Window light may come from the left and lay one soft shadow to the right. The cap and the body stay sharp. Crop so both are fully in frame, and keep the label as it is on your photo, or blank if you are only studying light. Do not invent a logo. Models improve a muted green into a brighter one and call it lighting. That shift is visible on a phone before any shadow work is. Customers who own the product notice the cap first. A wrong color is not a grade you can explain in the caption. Use the source photo as the reference, generate, compare, reject the new hue, and regenerate. If a realistic face is in the crop, label it as AI. A cartoon is a different case.",
    points: [
      { h: "Compare, do not remember", p: "Open the source photo beside the still on the phone. Caps, liquids, and boxes drift. Memory is generous. The photo is not. One mismatch is a new still, not a small flaw. Look at the cap before the shadow. Generate only from that photo, compare at arm's length, reject a cap that changed name, and regenerate. Keep the pack sharp. Crop out extra bottles in other colors, because the model will treat them as permission. The window stays on the left so you are judging color, not hunting for a second light." },
      { h: "Light may warm, it may not recolor", p: "A window can warm a white cap slightly. It cannot turn a white cap black or a dull green body emerald. If the name of the color changed, the still failed, and you can see the new name on a phone without a color picker. Compare the lit side and the shadow side. Both should still be the same object. Reject a shadow that is a different product. Regenerate with the source colors named and the light described only as a window camera-left. The label stays sharp and stays yours. Do not crop the cap off to hide a mismatch." },
      { h: "Do not ask for a richer color", p: "Richer, more premium, and more vibrant are how the pack leaves the factory. Describe the light. Leave the object alone. Invented colors are invented products, and an invented crest on the label is the same kind of invention. On a phone the premium pass is simply a different bottle. Generate from the photo, compare, reject the upgrade, and regenerate with those words deleted. One soft shadow may move. The hue may not. Crop to the single pack so a prettier neighbor cannot leak in." },
    ],
    example: "Reference: your photo of the real bottle, dull green body, white cap, true label. Generate with window light camera-left, one shadow to the right, cap and body sharp, crop tight on that one pack. Compare on a phone, photo beside still. Keep the still that only moved the shadow. Reject the still that made the body emerald and the cap black, or that redrew the label. Regenerate from the photograph with richer and premium deleted.",
    bridge: "Scro's product path starts from your photos so this comparison is possible. Approve the still only when the color matches. If the cap or the liquid changed, reject it and regenerate before motion. Then show one use and export 9:16. If a realistic face stays in frame, label it as AI. A cartoon is a different case.",
